    <div class="blog-body" style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.9; font-size: 1.05rem;">
        <p style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #f8fafc; margin-bottom: 1.75rem;">
            It was late in the night, about 3 AM, and like most nights I was still awake thinking about everything and nothing, until one very specific idea entered my head and took me away with it: <strong>what if there was an infinite board for the whole world?</strong>
        </p>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.75rem;">
            Nearly all the sites and tools we use today are separated into individual private rooms or accounts. A digital whiteboard that you open is only your own or a few of your friends, accessible by a link and invite. When you scroll through social media, each post is quickly gone. I wondered what it would be like if there was a single open space on the internet that went on forever – like one big digital board anyone could join and work on.
        </p>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem; font-weight: 600; color: #38bdf8; font-size: 1.15rem;">
            And that night was the beginning of Endless Sync (Endlessync).
        </p>

        <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); margin: 2.5rem 0;">

        <!-- Section: The Idea and the Original Version -->
        <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #f472b6; font-size: 1.6rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
                The Idea and the Original Version: Weptigo Infinity Canvas
            </h2>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                The idea of Endlessync is simple. Think of it as a huge infinite canvas, you can pan in any direction without hitting a wall. Within this board you can type what you are thinking, scribble and draw freely, import images and attach notes. But the big emphasis is the whole world has the exact same board in real time.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                If I create a private room or lock something it is exposed to everyone on that page. So if I log onto the site and create a note or drawing, it is instantly seen by whomever else is on the page and the digital interaction goes both ways: someone else in the world can see what I created and add to it, draw near to it, redraw over it, or erase some or all of it to create something new.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                But this version of Endlessync didn't just snap into existence. In fact, I created a version of this site around two years before the published version, when I first created this site under the name of <strong>"Weptigo Infinity Canvas"</strong>. Though that previous version was indexed very well on Google, and in AI searches, it was not my vision. There were quite a few issues and errors that I could not overcome at the time on a not-very-great computer that was being used to push two things at a time two years ago.
            </p>
        </section>

        <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); margin: 2.5rem 0;">

        <!-- Section: Rebuilding During Navratri 2026 -->
        <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #f472b6; font-size: 1.6rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
                Rebuilding During Navratri 2026: Sickness, Leaves, and Code
            </h2>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                And then was the Navratri of 2026 coming in the first week of October, 15 of October or so, I had thought of doing it once again. I was not well at that time and there were enough leaves, so I had few work and some free time.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                It was a lot of work to build. I mean, you can build a normal website, but an infinite board, where it updates live, is a whole other thing. When multiple people are drawing, adding images, and typing at the same time, it has to update those changes live, without lag or crashing the browser.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                I had to structure the system in such a way that only the part of the board being viewed was loaded, rather than trying to download the entire board and totally freezing the computer. Next was setting up the real-time connections to broadcast whenever someone moved an object, typed a word or drew a stroke to any other active user viewing the same spot. There were many bugs and failed tests, with objects not updating correctly and coordinates drifting. It took many hours of diagnosing the logic, testing the canvas output and tuning the speed of data transfer to make it into something that felt natural.
            </p>
        </section>

        <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); margin: 2.5rem 0;">

